  "image_name": "8c5b65b7926c13995e96cb8b7689f501.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes what “electrode interfaces” do in electrochemical systems and how they can be classified. At the top, several example technologies are sketched: water splitting and electrolysis, electroplating and electrometallurgy, reduction of species such as CO2 or nitrate, redox-mediator systems like sensors or flow batteries, and fuel-cell or battery reactions involving hydrogen, oxygen, or organic fuels. Together these examples show that the same basic interface concept appears across many electrochemical devices.\n\nThe larger lower schematic organizes the electrode interface from the bulk material to the electrolyte side. On the solid side, important factors include electronic structure, interactions, and charge transport or resistance within the electrode or current collector. At the interface, different contact types are highlighted, including solid/liquid, solid/gas, mixed solid/liquid/gas, and solid/solid boundaries. On the electrolyte side, the drawing emphasizes the electric double layer, with a compact Stern layer near a charged surface and a more diffuse ionic region farther away. Additional boxes indicate that catalytic behavior is shaped by surface reconstruction, core–shell or heterogeneous structures, binding at active sites, ion distribution, and transport of ions or reactants. Overall, the figure conveys that electrochemical performance emerges from coupled effects across the solid surface and adjacent electrolyte.",
